# Cupcake Project Context

This file is a compact reference for tools and contributors. It mirrors the canonical rules in `prompts/00-shared-context.md`. When the two disagree, the shared context file wins.

## Product

Cupcake is a multi-tenant SaaS platform for creating, sending, answering, and analyzing surveys and online forms. Customers build surveys with a visual drag-and-drop editor, deliver them through email, SMS, and WhatsApp, collect public responses through unique slugs, and analyze results in dashboards. The product is a realistic commercial foundation, not a disposable prototype.

## Language Rules

- All source code, code comments, README files, API documentation, seed documentation, developer docs, and inline technical docs MUST be in English.
- User-facing strings must be centralized or otherwise prepared for future internationalization.
- Spanish may be used only in external planning conversations, never in committed code or project docs.

## Stack

- Frontend: Vue 3 (Composition API) + Vite, Pinia, Vue Router.
- Frontend styling: shared global CSS files `theme.css`, `global.css`, `layout.css`, `utilities.css`.
- Backend: Node.js (current Active LTS, Node 24 at project start) + Express.
- Database: MongoDB via Mongoose.
- API: REST under `/api/v1`.
- Local infrastructure: Docker Compose for MongoDB.
- Package manager: pnpm workspaces, pinned in the root `package.json`.

## Application Name

The product name is configurable. Use `APP_NAME` on the backend and `VITE_APP_NAME` on the frontend. Do not hardcode "Cupcake" where the runtime name is meant.

## Architecture Rules

- Modular by domain.
- Business logic lives in services/repositories, not in controllers or route files.
- Backend modules separate routes, controllers, services, repositories, models, validation, and tests.
- Frontend features separate views, stores, services, reusable components, and feature-specific components.
- Prefer small, cohesive Vue components.
- Avoid duplicated CSS and duplicated API request logic.
- Use structured validation rather than ad hoc checks.
- Use consistent API response envelopes.
- Modules must be tenant-safe by default.

## Security Rules

- Hash passwords with bcrypt or argon2.
- Short-lived access tokens, secure refresh tokens; store refresh tokens server-side as hashes or revocable records.
- Rate-limit authentication and public form endpoints.
- Enable Helmet/security headers on Express.
- Sanitize and validate inputs; validate MongoDB ObjectId before querying.
- CORS configured via environment variables.
- Never expose stack traces in production; never log secrets, passwords, JWTs, refresh tokens, or full Authorization headers.

## Multi-Tenancy Rules

- Every business entity carries `tenantId` unless explicitly global.
- Super admins may have `tenantId: null`.
- Tenant isolation is enforced in backend services and repositories, not only in frontend filters.
- Public form routes resolve survey and tenant via published slug/version data.

## Roles

- `super_admin`, `client_admin`, `client_member`.
- Authorization is enforced in the backend.

## Survey Lifecycle

- Statuses: `draft`, `published`, `paused`, `archived`.
- Only `published` surveys accept responses.
- Publishing creates an immutable `survey_versions` record.
- Responses reference the exact `surveyVersionId` used at submission.
- Editing a published survey must not mutate historical published schemas.
- Duplicating a survey creates a new `draft`.

## API Response Envelope

Success:

```json
{ "success": true, "data": {}, "message": "Operation completed successfully" }
```

Error:

```json
{ "success": false, "error": { "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR", "message": "Invalid payload", "details": [] } }
```

## Default Ports

- Backend: `4000`
- Frontend: `5173`
- MongoDB: `27017`

## Execution Rules

- Run phases in numeric order from `prompts/`.
- Before each phase, load `prompts/00-shared-context.md` and the relevant phase file.
- Do not start the next phase until the current phase Definition of Done is satisfied.
- If a later phase reveals a missing foundation from an earlier phase, fix the foundation in the same branch instead of working around it.

## Final architecture (post Phase 17)

Backend modules live under `backend/src/modules/`: `auth`, `users`, `tenants`,
`surveys`, `responses`, `contacts`, `campaigns`, `public-forms`, `templates`,
`dashboard`, `transactions`, `admin`, `audit`, `health`. Cross-cutting code
lives under `backend/src/common/` (`auth`, `constants`, `errors`,
`middlewares`, `responses`, `utils`). The full route map is exposed at `GET
/api/v1` and documented in [backend/README.md](backend/README.md).

Frontend feature folders live under `frontend/src/views/` and
`frontend/src/features/`. Pinia stores under `frontend/src/stores/` (one per
domain) call the matching service in `frontend/src/services/`, which goes
through `apiClient.js` for refresh-token-aware fetch. Layouts live in
`frontend/src/layouts/`, route guards in `frontend/src/router/guards.js`, and
all user-facing strings in `frontend/src/i18n/{en,es}.js`.

## Phase 15–17 surfaces

- Campaign delivery is provider-agnostic. The `provider.factory` resolves
  `EMAIL_PROVIDER`, `SMS_PROVIDER`, `WHATSAPP_PROVIDER` to mock or Twilio /
  Twilio SendGrid adapters. Real adapters fail fast at construction time when
  the required credentials are missing. Provider responses are normalised
  into the recipient status model. See
  [docs/provider-architecture.md](docs/provider-architecture.md).
- Per-survey response limits live on `survey.settings.responseLimit` and are
  copied into `survey_versions.settings` at publish time so already-published
  surveys cannot be loosened by editing the live draft. Backend enforcement
  is authoritative via partial-unique indexes on `responses.metadata.*Hash`.
  See [docs/response-limits-and-settings.md](docs/response-limits-and-settings.md).
- Authenticated account settings live under `/api/v1/settings/*`. Password
  changes revoke active refresh tokens; locale preference is exposed through
  `/auth/me` and applied by the frontend i18n runtime.
- The dashboard shell uses a collapsible sticky sidenav, a sticky topbar, and
  the shared `AppTable` component for responsive horizontal/vertical
  scrolling with sticky headers.

## Deferred features

Intentionally deferred past Phase 18 and documented in
[docs/known-limitations.md](docs/known-limitations.md): password reset by
email, bulk contact import, real payment-provider integration, in-product
user management UI, webhook signature verification for the Twilio/SendGrid
callback routes, an email-verification flow for staged email changes, and
calling-session preservation on password change. Each surface either returns
a stable "temporarily unavailable" response or stays inert until its
configuration is provided — never a silent success.
